    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm also worried about the 'source' field and how we handle maps that seem to have been modified or added to by someone later on, but the original provenance is lost. Sometimes the markings or the way the ink looks is so strange or out-of-place that it feels like a different hand entirely, but we can't identify who or what it was. Is there a way to record those \"mysterious\" or anomalous additions in the metadata without just saying \"unknown\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In such cases, you can use the Dublin Core term \"Other version\" or \"Alternative version\" to indicate that there's a variation or modification of the map, without explicitly stating who made the changes. Alternatively, you could use the phrase \" Ancillary markings or annotations\" to provide context without implying authorship. This nuanced approach can help capture the complexity of the map's history.",
      "source": "target",
